Here are several JavaScript solutions to remove the "(age X)" pattern from your string:

## Method 1: Using Regular Expression (Recommended)
```javascript
function removeAge(str) {
    return str.replace(/\s*\(age\s+\d+\)\s*/g, '');
}

// Example usage:
const input = "july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK (1994-07-07)";
const result = removeAge(input);
console.log(result); // "july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)"
```

## Method 2: More Flexible Pattern (handles multiple digits and spaces)
```javascript
function removeAge(str) {
    return str.replace(/\s*\(age\s+\d+\)\s*/gi, '').trim();
}

// Test with different examples:
console.log(removeAge("july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK (1994-07-07)"));
// Output: "july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)"

console.log(removeAge("John Smith (age 25) New York"));
// Output: "John Smith New York"

console.log(removeAge("Data (AGE 123) More data"));
// Output: "Data More data"
```

## Method 3: Arrow Function (One-liner)
```javascript
const removeAge = str => str.replace(/\s*\(age\s+\d+\)\s*/gi, '').trim();

// Usage:
const text = "july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK (1994-07-07)";
console.log(removeAge(text));
```

## Method 4: With Additional Cleanup (removes extra spaces)
```javascript
function removeAge(str) {
    return str
        .replace(/\s*\(age\s+\d+\)\s*/gi, ' ')
        .replace(/\s+/g, ' ')
        .trim();
}

// This ensures no double spaces are left behind
```

## Regular Expression Breakdown:
- `\s*` - matches zero or more whitespace characters
- `\(` - matches literal opening parenthesis
- `age` - matches the word "age"
- `\s+` - matches one or more whitespace characters
- `\d+` - matches one or more digits
- `\)` - matches literal closing parenthesis
- `\s*` - matches zero or more whitespace characters
- `g` - global flag (replaces all occurrences)
- `i` - case-insensitive flag (optional)

The **Method 1** is the most straightforward and should work perfectly for your use case!

